<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hieronymus Bosch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1450–<a href="/years/1516">1516</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Jheronimus van Aken, Jheronimus Bosch, Bosch</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hieronymus Bosch, born Jheronimus van Aken, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> painter from Brabant and one of the most notable representatives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-netherlandish-painting">Early Netherlandish painting</a> school. He spent most of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/s-hertogenbosch">'s-Hertogenbosch</a>, which gave him his surname. His works, generally oil on oak wood, mainly illustrate religious concepts and narratives, with macabre depictions of hell, and were collected in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in his lifetime. Only about 25 paintings and eight drawings are confidently identified as his; his most acclaimed works are three triptych altarpieces, particularly The Garden of Earthly Delights. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pieter-bruegel-the-elder">Pieter Bruegel the Elder</a> was his best-known follower.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hieronymus-bosch.jpg" alt="Hieronymus Bosch" width="330" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Jacques Le Boucq · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jheronimus_Bosch_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Bosch's life or training. He left no letters or diaries, and what is known comes from brief mentions in the municipal records of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/s-hertogenbosch">'s-Hertogenbosch</a> and in the account books of the local religious confraternity, the Illustrious Brotherhood of Our Blessed Lady. His forefathers came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nijmegen">Nijmegen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, hence the name Van Aken. His birth date is estimated from a drawing, possibly a self-portrait, made shortly before his death.</p><p>His grandfather Jan van Aken was a painter, as were four of Jan's five sons, and his father Anthonius served as artistic adviser to the Brotherhood. Sometime between 1479 and 1481 he married Aleid Goyaerts van den Meervenne, who had inherited a house and land at Oirschot, and he later joined the Brotherhood, of about 40 influential citizens and 7,000 outer members.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Bosch painted mostly in oil on oak panels. He sometimes painted in a sketchy manner with a rough surface, unlike the traditional Early Netherlandish smooth finish. He did not date his works but seems to have signed several, and his signature appears on only seven surviving paintings. He made at least sixteen triptychs, eight of which survive intact. In the late 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> acquired many of his paintings, and the Prado holds The Garden of Earthly Delights and The Haywain Triptych.</p><p>In 1560 Felipe de Guevara described him as an inventor of monsters and chimeras. Recent scholars see his art as consistent with late medieval sermons and didactic literature, made to teach moral and spiritual truths. Art historians have also pointed to irony in The Garden of Earthly Delights.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The accounts of the Brotherhood record his death, and a memorial funeral mass was held in the church of Saint John on 9 August 1516. His work was collected in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in his lifetime and widely copied.</p><p>Scholars have given him fewer works over the years, helped by infrared reflectography of underdrawings. In early 2016 a small Temptation of St. Anthony in Kansas City was credited to Bosch himself after study by the Bosch Research and Conservation Project, which has also questioned The Seven Deadly Sins in the Prado.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hieronymus Bosch” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
